WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.209
Regulatory capture.

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:04.784
When regulators serve industry.

00:00:04.784 --> 00:00:16.113
Canada's regulatory bodies — the C.R.T.C., the Canada Energy Regulator, O.S.F.I., the Patented Medicine Prices Review Board — are supposed to protect the public interest from industry excess.

00:00:16.113 --> 00:00:28.272
In practice, the revolving door between regulators and industry, industry-funded regulatory processes, and appointee selection from industry ranks ensures that regulatory outcomes consistently favour the industries being regulated.

00:00:28.272 --> 00:00:33.079
Canadians pay some of the highest telecommunications prices in the developed world.

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:36.420
Pipeline reviews favour the companies seeking approval.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.023
Bank stress tests are designed by the banks being tested.

00:00:40.023 --> 00:00:43.477
Drug pricing mechanisms fail to contain costs.

00:00:43.477 --> 00:00:49.090
Each case is documented from official appointments, regulatory decisions, and outcome data.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.806
The four major captured regulators.

00:00:51.806 --> 00:00:55.715
C.R.T.C.: telecommunications and broadcasting.

00:00:55.715 --> 00:00:59.261
C.E.R.: energy pipelines and exports.

00:00:59.261 --> 00:01:02.999
O.S.F.I.: banks and financial institutions.

00:01:02.999 --> 00:01:06.420
P.M.P.R.B.: patented medicine prices.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.522
The three mechanisms of regulatory capture.

00:01:09.522 --> 00:01:14.534
The revolving door: regulators come from and return to the industries they regulate.

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:20.420
Industry funding: regulatory processes are funded by cost recovery from the regulated entities.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:29.227
Asymmetric access: industry has unlimited resources to participate in regulatory proceedings while public interest groups operate on limited budgets.

00:01:29.227 --> 00:01:31.340
The capture architecture.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.613
Regulatory capture is the intersection of institutional capture and democratic deficit.

00:01:36.613 --> 00:01:39.511
The regulators are appointed by the government.

00:01:39.511 --> 00:01:44.590
The government is influenced by the industries through lobbying and campaign contributions.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.329
The regulators serve the industries.

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:49.386
The industries profit.

00:01:49.386 --> 00:01:55.534
The public pays higher prices, receives weaker environmental protection, and has no effective recourse.

00:01:55.534 --> 00:02:00.659
The accountability scorecard shows that oversight bodies cannot enforce against government.

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:06.045
Regulatory capture shows that regulatory bodies do not enforce against industry either.

00:02:06.045 --> 00:02:14.761
Both represent the same structural architecture: institutions designed for accountability that produce outcomes aligned with the interests they are supposed to constrain.
